1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,067
I opened my own A-B test, and Flagsmith logged me under a headline I never saw.

2
00:00:06,067 --> 00:00:08,721
It wasn't a bug in my code.

3
00:00:08,721 --> 00:00:13,650
It was a cache, set up the way a lot of sites are.

4
00:00:13,650 --> 00:00:16,683
This giant switch is a real Flagsmith flag.

5
00:00:16,683 --> 00:00:20,096
Turn it off, and the cache stops sharing pages.

6
00:00:20,096 --> 00:00:22,750
Five seconds later, everyone is recorded correctly.

7
00:00:22,750 --> 00:00:27,300
Turn it back on, and about half the visits are wrong again.

8
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:29,408
Here's how it happens.

9
00:00:29,408 --> 00:00:33,279
Visitor A loads the page, and the cache keeps a copy.

10
00:00:33,279 --> 00:00:36,798
Visitor B gets that copy, so B reads headline A.

11
00:00:36,798 --> 00:00:43,133
But Flagsmith assigned B to headline B, so B's click is credited to a headline B never saw.

12
00:00:43,133 --> 00:00:46,300
And the split still looks like a perfect fifty-fifty.

13
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:52,014
To measure the damage, I ran a controlled experiment with a known answer:

14
00:00:52,014 --> 00:00:57,800
three thousand visitors per run, where people who see headline B really do click more.

15
00:00:57,800 --> 00:01:02,043
Behind the cache, forty-five to forty-seven percent of exposures were mislabelled.

16
00:01:02,043 --> 00:01:07,057
The real effect shrank to almost nothing, and in one run it reversed.

17
00:01:07,057 --> 00:01:11,300
Flagsmith gave the better headline a thirty-seven percent chance to win.

18
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:16,083
A sample-ratio check can't catch this, because the split stays balanced.

19
00:01:16,083 --> 00:01:17,938
So I added one event:

20
00:01:17,938 --> 00:01:21,650
rendered B fires whenever headline B is actually on screen.

21
00:01:21,650 --> 00:01:26,846
In a healthy test, zero percent of visitors logged as A ever see B.

22
00:01:26,846 --> 00:01:31,300
With the cache bug, it's thirty-nine point six percent, right inside Flagsmith.

23
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:40,458
The whole study was pre-registered before any data, replicated across three seeds, and every result is reported, including the misses.

24
00:01:40,458 --> 00:01:44,265
Along the way, I reproduced an open Flagsmith issue:

25
00:01:44,265 --> 00:01:52,300
the sample-ratio value comes back empty when variant weights are zero, which is exactly what the setup guide uses.

26
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:57,319
This is Cached Out, built on Flagsmith Experimentation for Toggle Wars, Track One.

27
00:01:57,319 --> 00:02:03,300
Open the live demo, flip the switch, and find out whether a cache has ever lied to you.

